Regression test for the if-multi-child variable collision. When an `if` body
holds more than one statement under a single parent slot, every child rendered
into the same `<innerVar>` and clobbered its predecessors; worse, any child
that emitted an `<innerVar>Children` of its own -- every container does --
collided with its sibling, and the generated Go failed to compile with
"redeclared in this block".

The window body now flows through WalkLowered, and the declarative lowering
pass emits an AppendChild for every NodeInst declared inside a conditional or
loop body, threaded back to the surrounding parent: each of the three
`text(...)` widgets is constructed *and* added to the vbox. The vbox is __n3
and the labels are __n0..__n2, allocated in pass one. None of the labels
escapes __renderSlot0 -- created, added and tracked in m.__slot0 all within
that scope -- so the node-escape lowering pass emits them as distinct
function-local variables, which is what rules out the original collision.

-- app.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"

output { go { fyne(main=true) } }

window #app(title="t") {
    var visible = true

    vbox {
        if visible {
            text(value="A")
            text(value="B")
            text(value="C")
        }
    }
}
-- out/go/fyne/model.go --
